Would that have been the same version in which roles and races were 
differentiated, so that "elf" wasn't a role unto itself? It seems to me 
that since the devteam evidently decided that only humans can be of more 
than one alignment, they would have had to allow multiple alignments of 
various roles, else the races would have been overly restricted in the 
roles they could choose.

> (Note that it's not just the alignments allowed for your race that
> restrict your choice of role, otherwise you'd be allowed gnomish
> Tourists.)

You seem to have anticipated my remark above, but I'm not sure I follow 
your logic here. You seemed to be saying in the first paragraph that the 
devteam may not value differentiation as much as I think, because they've 
allowed roles multiple alignments, thus causing a bit more "overlap" 
among roles. Here, you seem to be saying that the devteam kept some 
differentiation (by restricting roles) since natural race-alignment ties 
would have allowed certain race-role combinations that are nonetheless 
disallowed.

My take on all of the above is 1) the devteam have decided to allow for a 
broad range of role/race/alignment combinations, and 2) they nonetheless 
have decided not to allow every possible combination. The restrictions 
appear to be made based on the devteam's conception of each race and 
role. Apparently, a gnome in a hawaiian shirt carrying an expensive 
camara is viewed as absurd.

Anyway, the issue of "how many role/race/alignment combinations should be 
allowed?" is different from "should the alignments have discernable 
impact on gameplay?"

> Now if I play a Lawful Valkyrie and get
> Excalibur, I can't help feeling like I'm cheating a little.

Yes, I'd prefer that dipping for Excalibur be only for Knights again, but 
lawfuls in general would have to be given something in return for losing 
that bonus.

[make Monks physically incapable of eating meat?]
> >> 
> >Yes, but that would be a rather ugly kludge, wouldn't it? Simply 
> >forbidding things within the game isn't generally The Nethack Way. Better 
> >to allow, but cause a trade-off worth thinking about.
> 
> Monks are practically disallowed from wearing body armour by the -20
> to-hit penalty; is that The Nethack Way?
> 
Ah, but "practically disallowed" and actually disallowed are two very 
different things. The game allows you to put the armor on, and then 
charges you a penalty for wearing it. That's exactly the type of trade-
off I was talking about. At high experience levels, a monk can wear body 
armor and still hit, at which point -20 to hit vs. a 14 point boost in AC 
(for +5 dsm) is "worth thinking about," even if its merely for a conduct.

Similarly, I'm not suggesting that lawfuls be *unable* to kill peacefuls 
or chaotics *unable* to pay for things in a shop; just be assessed 
penalties that are actually meaningful.

